{:edb/db-before-t 1002 :edb/db-after-t 1003 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534315 50 #inst "2026-10-11T19:05:32.487Z" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186161672 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two pills is drawn from a population. The population has pills of two different active ingredient concentrations, $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $\\textrm{15}\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$, distributed in the ratio $1:2$. Calculate $\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]$." 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186161672 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two pills is drawn from a population. The population has pills of two different active ingredient concentrations, $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $\\textrm{15}\\,\\textrm{mg/ml},$ distributed in the ratio $1:2.$ Calculate $\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}].$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186161672 1074 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pills in the population have concentrations of $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$, and these are distributed in the ratio $1:2$. Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of\n\n$$\n1+2 = 3\n$$\n\npills with only the following concentrations:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ &= \\frac{1}{3}∑_(i)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(30 + 2 \\cdot 15) \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{3} \\\\\n&= 20mg/ml\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - μ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(30^{2} + 2 \\cdot 15^{2}) - 20^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(900 + 450) - 400 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 1350 - 400 \\\\\n&= 450 - 400 \\\\\n&= 50(mg/ml)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] =\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} = \\dfrac{50}{2} = 25\\,(\\textrm{mg/ml})^2\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186161672 1074 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pills in the population have concentrations of $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+2 = 3$ pills with only the following concentrations:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(30+2\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{mg/ml},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({30}^{2}+2\\cdot {15}^{2})-{20}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(900+450)-400 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1\\,350-400 \\\\  & =450-400 \\\\  & =50\\,(\\text{mg/ml})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] =\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} = \\dfrac{50}{2} = 25\\,(\\textrm{mg/ml})^2.\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186161673 1071 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{200}$ is a sample of size $n=200$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu = 23$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 4$, calculate the standard error of $\\overline{X}$. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186161673 1071 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{200}$ is a sample of size $n=200$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu = 23$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 4,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overline{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186161673 1074 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 4\n$$\n\nand the sample size is $n=200$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X^(―)] &= \\frac{4}{200} \\\\\n&= 0.02\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{0.02} \\approx 0.14\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186161673 1074 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is $\\sigma^2 = 4$ and the sample size is $n=200,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{200} \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{0.02} \\approx 0.14 .\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186161674 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ of $n$ coconuts is drawn from a population. The population has coconuts of two different weights, $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb}$, distributed in the ratio $2:3$. Determine the smallest sample size required to give a standard error of less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}$." 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186161674 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ of $n$ coconuts is drawn from a population. The population has coconuts of two different weights, $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb},$ distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Determine the smallest sample size required to give a standard error of less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186161674 1074 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coconuts in the population have weights $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb}$, and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3$. Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of\n\n$$\n2+3 = 5\n$$\n\ncoconuts with only the following weights:\n\n$$\n1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ &= \\frac{1}{5}∑_(i)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(2 \\cdot 1.5 + 3 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(3 + 6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{5} \\\\\n&= 1.8lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{5}∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - μ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(2 \\cdot 1.5^{2} + 3 \\cdot 2^{2}) - 1.8^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(16.5) - 3.24 \\\\\n&= 3.3 - 3.24 \\\\\n&= 0.06lb^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma= \\sqrt{0.06}\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.06}{n}}\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{n}} & < 0.02 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{\\sqrt{n}} & < 0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.02^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.0004}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\sqrt{150} \\\\ n & > 150\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=151$." 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186161674 1074 "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coconuts in the population have weights $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+3 = 5$ coconuts with only the following weights:\n\n$$\n1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 1.5+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =1.8\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot {1.5}^{2}+3\\cdot {2}^{2})-{1.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(16.5)-3.24 \\\\  & =3.3-3.24 \\\\  & =0.06\\,{\\text{lb}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma= \\sqrt{0.06},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.06}{ n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{{0.02}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.0004}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{150} \\\\ n & >150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=151.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418456 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418457 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418458 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418459 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418454 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418455 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186162169 1050 1020.0 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186163326 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$, then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$. Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X^(―)] &= Var[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{n})^{2} \\cdot Var[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot Var[X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot (Var[X_{1}] + Var[X_{2}] + ⋯ + Var[X_{n}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot (σ^{2} + σ^{2} + ⋯ + σ^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot nσ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$. What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186163326 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186163333 1064 "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$, then the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186163333 1064 "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186163334 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186163334 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. We know that the sample mean\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$.\n\nHowever, it should be noted that each $X_i$ is *also* an unbiased estimator of the population mean! Recall that, by assumption\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nIn what sense is $\\overline{X}$ a \"better\" estimator of $\\mu$ than, say, $X_1$? The answer lies in their respective variances.\n\nAs the sample size $n$ becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of $\\overline{X}$ become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as $n\\to\\infty$,\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\to 0, \\qquad \\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\to 0\n$$\n\nSo, when the sample size $n$ is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$. Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that $\\overline{X}$ will give good estimates for $\\mu$. The individual sample elements do not have this property since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean $\\mu$ as the sample size $n$ increases." 13194139534315 17592186045417 false] [17592186418418 1080 "3\\,300" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418418 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418418 1083 #uuid "0a9b7d3d-e043-540f-ab15-0ac4204800d5" 13194139534315 17592