417 true] [17592186418454 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =5$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.02.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418454 1072 #uuid "0c52d3d8-d8c0-58d1-b731-d5f1789535ad" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418454 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >100 \\\\ n & >{100}^{2} \\\\ n & >10\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=10\\,001.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418451 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418455 1069 "q-143483" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418455 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ people is drawn from a population. The population has people of two different weights, $78\\,\\text{kg}$ and $90\\,\\text{kg},$ distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418455 1072 #uuid "960c78a3-6eed-5896-a952-b3269c984b54" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418455 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the people in the population have weights $78\\,\\text{kg}$ and $90\\,\\text{kg},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+1=3$ people only with the following weights:\n\n$$\n78\\,\\text{kg},\\,78\\,\\text{kg},\\,90\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 78+90) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(156+90) \\\\  & =\\frac{246}{3} \\\\  & =82\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot {78}^{2}+{90}^{2})-{82}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(20\\,268)-6\\,724 \\\\  & =6\\,756-6\\,724 \\\\  & =32\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{32},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.1\\,\\text{kg}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{n}} & <0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{32}}{0.1} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{32}{{0.1}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3\\,200} \\\\ n & >3\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=3\\,201.$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418448 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418456 1069 "q-143402" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418456 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two cans is drawn from a population. The population has cans of two different volumes, $0.5\\,\\text{L}$ and $\\text{1}\\,\\text{L},$ distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Calculate $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418456 1072 #uuid "97d17e06-c282-5844-bc81-dad74b21692f" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418456 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the cans in the population have volumes $0.5\\,\\text{L}$ and $1\\,\\text{L},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+3=5$ cans only with the following volumes:\n\n$$\n0.5\\,\\text{L},\\,0.5\\,\\text{L},\\,1\\,\\text{L},\\,1\\,\\text{L},\\,1\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\text{L},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot {0.5}^{2}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-{0.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3.5)-0.64 \\\\  & =0.7-0.64 \\\\  & =0.06\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.06}{2}=0.03\\,{\\text{L}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418453 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418457 1069 "q-143398" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418457 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{80}$ is a sample of size $n=80$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =-2$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10,$ calculate the value of $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418457 1072 #uuid "ad0a7d7a-199c-5de0-b29b-e8636043b58a" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418457 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10$ and the sample size $n=80,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{10}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418452 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418458 1069 "q-143434" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418458 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four cellphones is drawn from a population. The population has cellphones of two different screen sizes, $4.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $6\\,\\text{in},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418458 1072 #uuid "aef7c5cc-0e11-515b-bc29-258a1089ae81" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418458 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the cellphones in the population have screen sizes of $4.5\\,\\text{in}$ and $6\\,\\text{in},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ cellphones only with the following screen sizes:\n\n$$\n4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,4.5\\,\\text{in},\\,6\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 4.5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(18+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\text{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {4.5}^{2}+{6}^{2})-{4.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(117)-23.04 \\\\  & =23.4-23.04 \\\\  & =0.36\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.36}{4}=0.09\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.09}=0.3\\,\\text{in}.\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418449 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418459 1069 "q-143428" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418459 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four bills is drawn from a population. The population has bills of two different denominations, $$5$ and $$10,$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418459 1072 #uuid "de928809-8125-57ca-bec0-eb6d36dc320c" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true] [17592186418459 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bills in the population have denominations $$5$ and $$10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bills only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$5,\\,$5,\\,$5,\\,$10\\,$10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(15+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =$7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {5}^{2}+2\\cdot {10}^{2})-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(275)-49 \\\\  & =55-49 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{1.5}≈$1.22.\n$$" 13194139534315 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0a9b7d3d-e043-540f-ab15-0ac4204800d5" 17592186418418 "answer-129fb933-2e4f-545e-993a-52c41611ff34" 17592186418419 "answer-2f90f43c-2a1a-5c25-8c24-c29c8d08d6ef" 17592186418420 "answer-35227a5a-7d12-5671-b51d-fa38508a5e1c" 17592186418421 "answer-353a41d3-498d-5a8a-a9e6-efadecfbdf97" 17592186418422 "answer-382b8d60-342e-5733-8bbd-fe774dd9f833" 17592186418423 "answer-397deda3-79ec-54be-8324-ca298ce871b7" 17592186418424 "answer-4d08f4df-3304-5238-9ee4-c8a784de2239" 17592186418425 "answer-51e57186-4049-585f-8889-b1eedaf